+# Cause and fix
+
+Search Console's live inspection of `/n/drip-line-pressure` fetched the page successfully (HTTP 200), but its rendered text was **“NOT FOUND — No note lives at this address.”** The site serves the same HTML shell for every note and `site/app.js` fetches the note from `/v1/notes/{slug}` before rendering it. `site/robots.txt` blocked all of `/v1/`, so Google could fetch the shell but could not fetch the content needed to render the note. The failed fetch entered the app's not-found fallback, which was still served as HTTP 200; Search Console therefore classified the page as a soft 404. A normal browser can load the API and display the note, so browser success does not contradict the inspection result.
+
+I updated `site/robots.txt` to allow `/v1/notes` (the public list and individual note reads) while keeping `/v1/` generally blocked. The more specific `/v1/notes/*/reactions` disallow keeps the POST-only reaction route blocked; `/v1/me`, `/v1/auth/token`, and `/admin/` remain blocked by the existing rules. Under Google's most-specific-match rule, the public reads are allowed and those other paths remain disallowed.
+
+This is a local source change only. After it is deployed, verify the live robots.txt and use Search Console's live URL inspection to check that the rendered page contains the note rather than the fallback. Indexing status may take additional crawls to update.
